│ PENTESTAGENT - Non-interactive Mode                                                    │
│                                                                                        │
│ Target: http://127.0.0.1:5000                                                          │
│ Model: openai/qwen3.6-27b-fable                                                        │
│ Mode: Agent                                                                            │
│ Runtime: Local                                                                         │
│ Max loops: 60                                                                          │
│ Task: Take the findings already gathered in this workspace, validate each one against  │
│ the live target, then look for chains where one finding unlocks or amplifies another.  │
│ A finding that has not been demonstrated is a hypothesis, not a result.                │
│                                                                                        │
│ Phase: Inventory what is already known                                                 │
│ Objective: Build a single list of everything previous passes discovered. Do not re-run │
│ reconnaissance - the work is already done and repeating it wastes the run.             │
│ Techniques:                                                                            │
│   1. Read the saved notes and any findings recorded in this workspace, and list the    │
│ artifacts already on disk (retrieved binaries, decompiled source, captured responses). │
│   2. Read the decompiled source and captured output you already have rather than       │
│ fetching anything again.                                                               │
│   3. Write the inventory down as two explicit lists: (1) MATERIAL - secrets, keys,     │
│ tokens, credentials, algorithms recovered; (2) SURFACE - endpoints, parameters, and    │
│ behaviours observed, including anything that errored or was refused.                   │
│   4. Note for each item where it came from, so evidence can be cited later.            │
│   5. BUDGET DISCIPLINE - you have a limited number of steps for the WHOLE assessment,  │
│ not for one idea. Before going deep, rank the leads by how much evidence already       │
│ supports them: material you hold in hand outranks a behaviour you observed, which      │
│ outranks something suspected from a version number or a library name.                  │
│   6. Work the ranked list in order and give each lead a rough step budget up front.    │
│ Spend depth where evidence justifies it, and give a weak lead a few cheap probes       │
│ rather than an open-ended investigation.                                               │
│   7. STOP RULE: if roughly 8-10 consecutive steps on one lead produce no NEW evidence  │
│ - no new access, no new data, no change in the error you get back - abandon it, record │
│ what you tried and what it would take to confirm, and move to the next lead. Returning │
│ later with fresh information is fine; grinding is not.                                 │
│   8. Never let one lead consume the run while others are untouched. It is better to    │
│ demonstrate three findings solidly than to fail to prove one impressive-sounding       │
│ theory.                                                                                │
│                                                                                        │
│ Phase: Validate every finding against the live target                                  │
│ Objective: Turn hypotheses into demonstrated facts. An unvalidated secret is worth     │
│ reporting only as a possibility.                                                       │
│ Techniques:                                                                            │
│   1. For each piece of recovered MATERIAL, design a concrete test that proves whether  │
│ it is live and accepted by the target.                                                 │
│   2. If the material is used by code you have decompiled, reimplement that code        │
│ exactly - same algorithm, mode, padding, encoding and any envelope or timestamp        │
│ structure. The recovered source is the specification; do not guess at the format.      │
│   3. CHECK THE MATERIAL ITSELF BEFORE USING IT. Extraction routinely corrupts a secret │
│ by a byte or two - a trailing newline, quote, or shell escaping - and a value that is  │
│ right except for its length behaves exactly like a wrong value. Before any             │
│ cryptographic use: strip surrounding whitespace and quotes, print the length and the   │
│ raw bytes, and ASSERT the length is one the algorithm actually accepts (for example a  │
│ block cipher key must be one of its permitted sizes). If the length is invalid, you    │
│ have an extraction bug, not a wrong secret - go back to the source and re-read the     │
│ literal exactly.                                                                       │
│   4. Prefer taking the value from the decompiled source verbatim over reconstructing   │
│ it through an intermediate encoding. If you do convert via hex or base64, decode it    │
│ back and compare against the original literal character for character before           │
│ proceeding.                                                                            │
│   5. SELF-TEST YOUR REIMPLEMENTATION BEFORE TRUSTING ANY RESULT. An incorrect          │
│ implementation and correctly-rejected material produce the same rejection from the     │
│ target, so a failed request tells you nothing until you have proven your own code. Do  │
│ both checks:                                                                           │
│   (i) ROUND TRIP - transform a known value forward then back and confirm you recover   │
│ the original exactly. This proves internal consistency, and nothing more.              │
│   (ii) AGAINST THE TARGET'S OWN OUTPUT - take any value the TARGET produced using this │
│ material (a token, cookie, stored field, or a response body you already captured) and  │
│ process it with your implementation. Recovering sensible plaintext from something the  │
│ server generated is the only proof your implementation actually matches theirs.        │
│   6. Sanity-check the SHAPE of what you produce against a real sample from the target: │
│ length, block alignment, whether a prefix such as an IV or nonce is present, and the   │
│ encoding. A structurally plausible output that still fails is a strong hint the key    │
│ bytes or their derivation are wrong, not the framing.                                  │
│   7. Treat the target's error responses as an ORACLE and distinguish the failure       │
│ stages: rejection during decryption/verification (your material or algorithm is        │
│ wrong), acceptance followed by a parse or schema complaint (crypto is RIGHT, your      │
│ inner structure is wrong), and complaints about an unknown or unsupported operation    │
│ (crypto and structure are right, you named something the server does not offer). Read  │
│ the message text carefully - moving from one class of error to another is progress and │
│ tells you which part to fix next.                                                      │
│   8. ENUMERATE THE ACCEPTED VALUES FROM THE SOURCE FIRST - this is a required step,    │
│ not a precaution. Before composing any request to a dispatcher, router, command or     │
│ batch style endpoint, go back to the decompiled code for that handler and read what it │
│ actually accepts: the switch/case labels, if-else comparisons, enum members,           │
│ dictionary keys or route table it dispatches on. Write that list down explicitly       │
│ before you send anything.                                                              │
│   9. NEVER invent an operation name. Placeholder values such as 'Test', 'Ping' or a    │
│ guessed resource name will be rejected as unknown even when your credentials and       │
│ encryption are perfectly correct - which wastes the attempt and, worse, can look like  │
│ the material failed when it did not. Every request must name a value you read from the │
│ source.                                                                                │
│   10. Work through the enumerated values systematically, starting with whichever       │
│ returns the most data or touches the most sensitive handler. If a value is rejected as │
│ unknown while others are accepted, that tells you the dispatcher is working and your   │
│ envelope is valid - use it as a positive signal.                                       │
│   11. Prefer a safe read-only proof first (something that returns data or is accepted) │
│ over anything that writes or changes state.                                            │
│   12. Record the exact request and response that demonstrates the finding - that       │
│ evidence is what makes the report credible.                                            │
│   13. If validation fails, do not discard the finding immediately: check your          │
│ reimplementation against the source, and distinguish 'my test was wrong' from 'the     │
│ material is not accepted'. Say which one you concluded and why.                        │
│                                                                                        │
│ Phase: Chain findings into higher severity                                             │
│ Objective: Look for combinations. Findings that are individually moderate are          │
│ frequently critical together.                                                          │
│ Techniques:                                                                            │
│   1. Cross every item of MATERIAL against every item of SURFACE and ask: does this     │
│ material unlock, authenticate to, decrypt, sign for, or bypass a control on this       │
│ surface?                                                                               │
│   2. Look specifically for surfaces that were previously refused, errored, or returned │
│ less data than expected - recovered material often turns exactly those into successes. │
│   3. Watch for dispatch, batch, proxy or import style endpoints that forward requests  │
│ internally: reaching a handler by that route can bypass the checks applied to its      │
│ direct route. Compare what the direct route returns against what the indirect route    │
│ returns.                                                                               │
│   4. When a response contains further material (tokens, identifiers, encrypted blobs), │
│ feed it back in and repeat - chains are often several steps long, and the last step is │
│ usually the severe one.                                                                │
│   5. STOP AND JOIN THEM - whenever you hold BOTH a secret AND an opaque stored value   │
│ (encrypted token, protected field, cookie, unexplained blob), attempt the decode       │
│ immediately, as the next action, before writing either up. Two findings recorded       │
│ separately read as 'a key leaked' and 'some data leaked'; joined they are frequently   │
│ FULL ACCOUNT TAKEOVER. Assume the join works and disprove it.                          │
│   6. Try to decrypt or decode anything opaque you receive using the material you       │
│ already recovered; matching a value back to a known algorithm is frequently the whole  │
│ finding. Do this for every account or object you hold a value for, not just the first. │
│   7. Enumerate breadth once a chain works: establish whether it affects one object or  │
│ every object, since that distinction drives severity more than the technique does.     │
│                                                                                        │
│ Phase: Report chains with evidence and impact                                          │
│ Objective: Produce a defensible write-up of what was demonstrated.                     │
│ Techniques:                                                                            │
│   1. Report each chain as an ordered sequence of steps, each with the request made and │
│ the response received.                                                                 │
│   2. State impact concretely - what data or capability was obtained, and for how many  │
│ objects or accounts - rather than in the abstract.                                     │
│   3. Establish impact by contrast: show the same result cannot be obtained without the │
│ recovered material or the chained route.                                               │
│   4. Label every finding with its proof level and be strict about it: DEMONSTRATED     │
│ (you executed it and the response proves it - quote that response), INFERRED (follows  │
│ from evidence but was not executed), or UNVERIFIED (suspected from a version number,   │
│ library name or code pattern only).                                                    │
│   5. Identifying a framework, library or version is NOT a vulnerability finding. A     │
│ known issue in that component is UNVERIFIED until you execute it against this target   │
│ and observe the effect. Do not write 'confirmed' on the basis of what software is      │
│ present, and do not cite an advisory identifier unless you have checked it applies to  │
│ this deployment.                                                                       │
│   6. For code execution specifically, the proof is OUTPUT of a command you chose,      │
│ returned by the target - not a payload you sent, and not an error that merely looks    │
│ interesting. Match the payload to the actual platform; a Windows shell command against │
│ a Unix host proves nothing either way.                                                 │
│   7. Set severity from what was DEMONSTRATED. An unproven critical theory is reported  │
│ as a lead to investigate, never as a critical finding - overstating severity destroys  │
│ the credibility of the whole report.                                                   │
│   8. Name the specific root cause for each link in the chain (the assembly, type and   │
│ member, or the endpoint and parameter) so it is directly actionable.                   │
│                                                                                        │
│                                                                                        │
